WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.953
javac and kotlinc don't produce machine code.

2
00:00:05.953 --> 00:00:14.340
They produce bytecode: small instructions for the JVM, which HotSpot turns into machine code while the program runs.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.050
This is the loop body of settle, which adds up a batch of payments.

4
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.549
Bytecode 20 is the call to feeCents.

5
00:00:21.549 --> 00:00:23.700
Bytecode 31 tests for a refund.

6
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.709
Nothing is compiled at first.

7
00:00:25.709 --> 00:00:34.508
Every method starts in the interpreter, a loop inside the JVM that reads one bytecode instruction at a time and carries it out.

8
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.897
Meanwhile the JVM counts the calls, and the jumps back to the top of each loop.

9
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.554
At about 200, a compiler thread compiles the method with C1, in the background.

10
00:00:46.554 --> 00:00:49.176
C1 is quick, and optimizes a little.

11
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:56.048
Its code also records a profile: which way each branch went, and which receiver types reached each call.

12
00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:02.163
About 5,000 calls later, C2 compiles it again, slowly and hard, from that profile.

13
00:01:02.163 --> 00:01:09.653
The JVM raises these thresholds when the compile queues are long, so treat them as orders of magnitude.

14
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.660
C2 bets on the profile.

15
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:17.358
Every receiver was a Card, so it inlines Card's feeCents behind a cheap class check.

16
00:01:17.358 --> 00:01:22.920
The refund branch never ran, so C2 leaves it out, and puts an uncommon trap there.

17
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.443
Then a refund arrives.

18
00:01:24.443 --> 00:01:27.248
The trap fires, and the JVM deoptimizes.

19
00:01:27.248 --> 00:01:32.648
It rebuilds an interpreter frame from the compiled one, and carries on there.

20
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.994
The C2 code is made not entrant.

21
00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:39.725
The method profiles again, now with refunds, and gets compiled again.

22
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.503
A burst of this after a deploy is normal.

23
00:01:42.503 --> 00:01:46.064
A steady stream, hours after startup, is worth a look.
